  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1215 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nSaturday-Sunday:\nMuch of Saturday will be dry across the area ahead of the next\nsystem. Additionally, it appears increasing cloud cover does not\noccur until late afternoon and evening. As 850mb temperatures\napproach 9 to 11 C in the afternoon, surface highs will climb\ninto the lower 70s. This is 10 to 15 degrees above normal for \nmid/late November. This will be last of the much warmer \ntemperatures for the foreseeable future, with cooler air progged\nto filter in behind the frontal passage on Sunday. \n\nThe latest trends suggest scattered showers to overspread west\nto east Saturday night, with a gradual uptick in coverage\nthrough the overnight. Onset timing looks to be focused after\nmidnight, with the greatest coverage towards sunrise on Sunday\nmorning. Guidance captures minimal instability, with MUCAPE \naround 150-250 J/kg at best. With that being said, much of the \nactivity will be showery in nature, with a low chance (10%) for \na few embedded thunderstorms. In general, the best chances for \nseeing a stray thunderstorm will be focused north and west of \nInterstate 44. Additionally, this same area remains the focus \nfor highest rain chances (70-90%+) and potential amounts. NBM \nprobabilities suggest this area sees the best chances (70-90%) \nfor seeing greater than a quarter inch. However, probabilities \nfor greater a half inch drop off to 40-60% in this same area. \nThus, confidence remains fairly high that the highest amounts \nwill be limited to a quarter to half inch, with localized areas \na bit higher (but less than an inch). Further south and east of \nInterstate 44, amounts taper off a quarter inch or less. At this\ntime, no flooding or severe weather are expected. Any rainfall \nwill be beneficial given the ongoing drought conditions across \nthe area.\n\nAs the front surges in from the north, rain chances exit to the\neast through Sunday morning. Lingering cloud cover through\nSunday, with highs in the middle 50s.\n\nNext Week:\nEnsemble guidance is in fairly good agreement that cooler and\nmostly dry weather are expected for Thanksgiving week. Highs\nTuesday and beyond are progged to be in the 40s to around 50,\nwith overnight lows in the 20s. As mentioned in previous AFDs,\nthere is little to no PoPs in the forecast through at least\nThursday. Beyond Thursday, there is a bit more uncertainty in\nthe upper-level pattern.",
